course: dictionary
lesson: 5295
title: Words from the courtroom
level: primary-5
stars: 1
explain: A juror is one of the ordinary adult citizens who sit on a jury. A jury is a group of people who listen to a trial in a court and then decide if the accused person is guilty or not guilty. Jurors are not judges or lawyers; they are everyday people asked to be fair.
explain: Use the words around juror to work out its meaning. «There are twelve jurors» tells you they form a group. Then ask what they do: they listen to the evidence, the facts shown in court, and they decide. Swap your meaning into the sentence and check that it still makes sense.
explain: Alice in Wonderland is a story, so its jurors are strange: some are animals and birds. Real jurors are people. Carroll's story is a joke, so use it to learn the word, not to learn how real courts work.
fact: A juror is an ordinary adult citizen who serves on a jury.
fact: A jury listens to a trial and decides if the accused person is guilty or not guilty.
fact: In the UK, the USA, Canada, Australia, New Zealand and Ireland, adults can be called to be jurors.
fact: Jurors must be fair and listen carefully to the evidence.
fact: In Alice, the jurors write their names on slates «for fear they should forget them», which is a joke about juries that do not pay attention.
exercise: «There are twelve jurors, some animals and some birds.» In real life, who can be a juror? | Animals and birds | *Ordinary adult citizens | Only judges | Children under ten
exercise: A jury listens to a trial. What does it decide? | What colour the courtroom walls should be | What the judge will eat for lunch | *Whether the accused person is guilty or not guilty
exercise: Alice's story makes fun of something. What is it? | Rainy weather in the courtroom | *Juries that do not pay attention | Cats who sing songs | Very good spelling
test: «The twelve creatures in the jury-box are the jurors.» What does a juror do? | Wears a great wig and a crown | Writes names on slates for fun | *Listens to the evidence and decides guilty or not guilty
test: In real courts, what must jurors be? | *Fair | Loud | Silly | Lost
exam: What is a juror? | A judge who wears a wig | A lawyer who asks the questions | A king in a story | *A person who sits on a jury and decides if someone is guilty
